# PowerShell on Windows

The default shell here is PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`). Follow these conventions when running or proposing shell commands.

## Syntax

- Use `;` (or new lines) to chain statements. `&&` and `||` work in pwsh 7 but `;` is the conservative choice.
- Variables: `$name`, not `$NAME` or `${name}`. Path expansion: `$env:HOME`, `$env:USERPROFILE`.
- Quoting: prefer single quotes for literal strings. Use double quotes only when expansion is needed.
- Comparisons: `-eq`, `-ne`, `-lt`, `-gt`, `-match`. NOT `==`, `!=`.
- Globbing: `Get-ChildItem -Filter '*.ts'`. Pipelines use `|`.

## Commands

- List files: `Get-ChildItem` (alias `ls`, `dir`). Hidden: `-Force`.
- Read file: use the Read tool. If you must in a script, `Get-Content -Raw`.
- Make dir: `New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path ... -Force`.
- Symlink: `New-Item -ItemType SymbolicLink -Path link -Target real` (needs Developer Mode or admin).
- Env var (session): `$env:FOO = 'bar'`. Persistent user var: `[Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('FOO','bar','User')`.
- Download: `Invoke-WebRequest` (alias `iwr`). HTTP API: `Invoke-RestMethod`.

## Common mistakes to avoid

- Do not use `&&` casually; in PowerShell <7 it fails. Prefer `;` or explicit `if ($LASTEXITCODE -eq 0)`.
- Do not pipe to `xargs`. Use `ForEach-Object` (`%`) instead.
- Do not assume `which`, `touch`, `mkcd` are built-in; this dotfiles repo defines them, but other machines may not.
- Backslashes in strings need escaping or use of single quotes / forward slashes (which Windows accepts most places).

## When to switch to bash

Switch to bash / WSL only when:
- The user is explicitly working inside WSL.
- The repo's tooling (Makefile, shell scripts, hooks) requires bash and would be awkward to port.
- The command is a documented Linux-only one-liner the user pasted.
